# Error Handling Patterns

Error handling conventions for backend (NestJS) and frontend (React).

## Backend Error Handling

### Exception Types

```typescript
import {
  BadRequestException,     // 400 - Client errors, validation
  NotFoundException,        // 404 - Resource not found
  ForbiddenException,       // 403 - Permission denied, rate limits
  InternalServerErrorException  // 500 - Server errors
} from '@nestjs/common'

// Use descriptive messages
if (!form) {
  throw new BadRequestException('The form does not exist')
}

if (form.suspended) {
  throw new BadRequestException('The form is suspended')
}

if (!workspace) {
  throw new NotFoundException('The workspace does not exist')
}

if (remaining < 1) {
  throw new ForbiddenException('Limit exceeded. Please try again later.')
}
```

**Message Conventions**:
- Clear, user-friendly messages
- Start with "The" or "Invalid"
- Present tense: "does not exist" (not "did not exist")
- Sentence case

### Try-Catch Patterns

```typescript
// Log before re-throwing
try {
  const decrypted = this.endpointService.decryptToken(input.openToken)
  startAt = decrypted.timestamp
} catch (error) {
  this.logger.errorWithContext('Form submission failed: token error', error, {
    ...logContext,
    teamId: form.teamId
  })
  throw new BadRequestException('Invalid open token')
}

// Check error type before re-throwing
try {
  // ... code
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof BadRequestException) {
    throw error  // Re-throw original
  }
  this.logger.errorWithContext('Error context', error, logContext)
  throw new BadRequestException('Error message')
}

// Extract nested error messages
try {
  answers = fieldValuesToAnswers(fields, input.answers)
} catch (err: any) {
  const errorMessage = err?.response || err?.message || 'Validation failed'
  this.logger.errorWithContext('Validation error', err, logContext)
  throw new BadRequestException(errorMessage)
}
```

**Try-Catch Rules**:
- Always log before re-throwing
- Use `errorWithContext` for structured logging
- Check error type before re-throwing
- Extract error messages from nested errors
- Provide context in error logs

### Error Propagation

```typescript
// Re-throw critical errors
try {
  await this.endpointService.antiBotCheck(form.settings?.captchaKind, input)
} catch (error) {
  this.logger.warnWithContext('Bot check failed', logContext)
  throw error  // Re-throw
}

// Continue on non-critical errors
try {
  const isSpam = await this.endpointService.verifySpam({ answers, ip })
  if (isSpam) category = SubmissionCategoryEnum.SPAM
} catch (error) {
  this.logger.errorWithContext('Spam check failed', error, logContext)
  // Continue even if spam check fails
}
```

## Global Exception Filter

```typescript
@Catch()
export class AllExceptionsFilter implements ExceptionFilter {
  private readonly logger = new Logger(AllExceptionsFilter.name)

  catch(exception: Error, host: ArgumentsHost): void {
    const logContext = {
      method: request?.method,
      url: request?.url || request?.body?.operationName,
      ip: request?.ip,
      userId: request?.user?.id
    }

    // GraphQL context - log and re-throw
    if (helper.isValid(ctx?.res)) {
      if (!(exception instanceof HttpException)) {
        this.logger.errorWithContext('Unhandled exception', exception, logContext)
        throw exception
      }
      const status = exception.getStatus()
      if (status >= 500) {
        this.logger.errorWithContext('Server error', exception, logContext)
      } else if (status >= 400) {
        this.logger.warnWithContext(`Client error: ${exception.message}`, logContext)
      }
      throw exception
    } else {
      // HTTP context - handle normally
      if (!(exception instanceof HttpException)) {
        httpException = new InternalServerErrorException(exception.message)
        this.logger.errorWithContext('Unhandled exception', exception, logContext)
      }
      res.status(httpException.getStatus()).json(httpException.getResponse())
    }
  }
}
```

**Filter Rules**:
- Handle GraphQL and HTTP contexts differently
- Log all errors with context
- Re-throw GraphQL errors (handled by formatError)
- Return JSON for HTTP errors

## GraphQL Error Formatting

```typescript
// formatError in GraphQL config
formatError: (error: GraphQLError) => {
  const exception = error.extensions?.exception as any
  const statusCode = exception?.status || 500

  // Hide internal error details
  if (statusCode >= 500) {
    return {
      message: 'Internal server error',
      statusCode: 500
    }
  }

  // Return formatted error for client errors
  return {
    message: error.message,
    statusCode: exception?.status || 400,
    code: exception?.code,
    extensions: {
      ...error.extensions,
      exception: undefined  // Remove stack traces
    }
  }
}
```

## Validation Errors

### Frontend Validation

```typescript
// Form validation with rc-field-form
<Field
  name="email"
  rules={[
    { required: true, message: 'Please enter your email' },
    { type: 'email', message: 'Please enter a valid email' }
  ]}
>
  {(control, meta) => {
    const hasError = meta.errors.length > 0
    return (
      <div data-error={hasError ? '' : undefined}>
        <Input {...control} hasError={hasError} />
        {hasError && <div className="text-error">{meta.errors[0]}</div>}
      </div>
    )
  }}
</Field>
```

### Backend Validation

```typescript
// class-validator in GraphQL inputs
@InputType()
export class CreateFormInput {
  @Field()
  @IsNotEmpty()
  projectId: string

  @Field({ nullable: true })
  @IsOptional()
  @IsUrl()
  redirectUrl?: string

  @Field()
  @Min(1)
  @Max(100)
  quotaLimit: number
}
```

## Logging

### Logger Usage

```typescript
// Logger with context
this.logger.errorWithContext(message, error, context)
this.logger.warnWithContext(message, context)
this.logger.infoWithContext(message, context)

// Context object
const logContext = {
  formId: form.id,
  teamId: form.teamId,
  userId: user.id,
  ip: request.ip,
  method: request.method,
  url: request.url
}
```

## Frontend Error Handling

### Error Boundaries

```typescript
import { ErrorBoundary } from 'react-error-boundary'

<ErrorBoundary
  FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}
  onError={(error, info) => {
    console.error('Error:', error)
    console.error('Info:', info)
  }}
>
  <App />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

### API Error Handling

```typescript
// Apollo Client error handling
import { useRequest } from 'ahooks'

const { loading, error, runAsync } = useRequest(fetch, {
  manual: true
})

async function handleSubmit(values: any) {
  try {
    await runAsync(values)
    toast({ title: 'Success', message: 'Form submitted' })
  } catch (err: any) {
    toast({ title: 'Error', message: err.message || 'Something went wrong' })
  }
}

// Display errors
{error && !loading && (
  <div className="text-error text-sm">{error.message}</div>
)}
```

### Toast Notifications

```typescript
import { toast } from '@/components'

// Success
toast({ title: 'Success', message: 'Form created successfully' })

// Error
toast({ title: 'Error', message: error.message })

// Warning
toast({ title: 'Warning', message: 'Please save your changes' })
```

## Best Practices

**Backend**:
- Use appropriate exception types (BadRequest, NotFound, Forbidden)
- Always log errors before re-throwing
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Extract nested error messages
- Use structured logging with context
- Don't expose internal errors to clients

**Frontend**:
- Use error boundaries for component errors
- Display user-friendly error messages
- Use toast notifications for feedback
- Handle API errors gracefully
- Log errors to console in development

**Validation**:
- Validate on both frontend and backend
- Use class-validator for backend DTOs
- Use rc-field-form for frontend forms
- Provide clear validation messages

**Logging**:
- Log all errors with context
- Use appropriate log levels (error, warn, info)
- Don't log sensitive data (passwords, tokens)
- Include request context (user, IP, URL)

## Critical Gotchas

1. **Always log before re-throwing** - Errors get lost otherwise
2. **Check error type** - Use `instanceof` before re-throwing
3. **Extract nested messages** - Use `err?.response || err?.message`
4. **User-friendly messages** - Don't expose stack traces
5. **Context in logs** - Include formId, teamId, userId
6. **GraphQL vs HTTP** - Handle differently in exception filter
7. **Hide server errors** - Return generic message for 500 errors
